### GETTING SET UP ###

Put the included files in your IDL path, or run IDL from that
directory.

RPHOT makes use of the umrotse_idl and goddard IDL procedures, so
those should be in your IDL path as well.

There is a C file, local_sky_sub.c, which must be compiled as a shared
object. A .so file compiled on a linux Pentium 4 is included,
otherwise look at the .c file for instructions.

In the function "get_local_sky.pro", the variable "sopath" must be set
to the path for the .so file compiled above.

For ease of use, images in $base/image/ should have their .cobj files
located in in $base/prod/.

### RUNNING RPHOT ###

Start IDL
Start rphot with:

IDL> rphot,data

You will retain access to the IDL command line. The parameter "data"
should be undefined; it provides access to the data structure.

From the RPHOT "Load" menu, select "Choose Images..." This launches a
file finder which allows you to select images to work with. You can
select multiple images.

From the RPHOT "Load" menu, select "Choose Ref Image..." This launches
a file finder which allows you to select a single image to serve as
the reference image. You will use this image to select the target and
fiducial reference stars. It should be your best (deepest, best PSF)
image, and may be a coadd.

From the RPHOT "Object" menu, select "Choose Target..." This will load
and display the reference image, and open a window to select the
target. You may either enter the exact coordinates and click "Show",
or zoom in (drag a box in the image display, etc.) and click on a
target.

From the RPHOT "Object" menu, select "Choose Refstars..." This launch
a window similar to the one above. Use this to select nearby reference
stars. Avoid saturated objects, blends, etc. You may use the "Auto
Select" button to automatically choose refstar candidates. Click
"Select by Cursor" to toggle refstar selection.

From the RPHOT "Display" menu, select "Display Image..." This brings
up a window with the list of images to choose from. Before selecting
an new image, you should first edit the sky annulus pixel mask for the
reference image shown in the small lower left window if necessary. If
the object shown here is not the target (no green circle), switch the
top bar mode from "zoom" to "examine" and click on the target in the
main image. This mask will be propagated to each image. You can
add/remove pixels from the mask with the left/right mouse buttons one
by one. If you have a mouse wheel, you can add/remove a 3x3
section. Now click on the name of an image in the "Choose Image"
window. RPHOT will automatically locate the target, reference stars,
perform aperture photometry, and update the plot. Click through image
by image to look for problems, or click the "Do All" button to run
through everything.

Change the top bar from "zoom" to "examine" to change the plotting
options (eg. plot magnitudes instead of flux).

To calibrate the relative magnitudes to a given photometric system,
select "Calibrate..." from the RPHOT "Object" menu, then select a file
with the secondary standard star positions and magnitudes. This file
must have the following format:

R
        RA         DEC    Rmag   emag
 13.601000    1.177275  19.254  0.136
 13.601100    1.272470  19.056  0.162
 13.601683    1.174317  17.549  0.021
   ...          ...       ...    ...

Where the first line tells which filter you're calibrating to. To
convert data provided by Henden to this format, use "henden_list.pro"


*** Other features ***

In the "examine" mode, you can click on objects in the displayed image
to see them in the close up window and to plot their light curves

You can click on light curve points to bring up the corresponding
image.

To set the burst time for a GRB:

IDL> (*data).mjd=julday(10,6,2004,12,18,08)-2400000.5d

To bin data points, first indicate which points are to be in which bin
by setting (*(*data).images).sumind to an array with the bin indices:

IDL> (*(*data).images).sumind=fix(indgen(n_elements(*(*data).images))/10)

Set the index to -1 for any images you don't care to sum (this will
also remove them from the unbinned plots). Then toggle the binning
with:

IDL> (*data).dosum=1  ;; binning on
IDL> (*data).dosum=0  ;; binning off

Re-plot the data to see the change.

